[ghudson@CIE.UOREGON.EDU (Greg Hudson) writes:]
[] 1. In most cases, I do use shift- keys to move; I'm not sure whether I
[] *can* use control keys with the numbered keypad, but I don't believe I can.

I think that g-<move> is equivalent to <ctrl>-<move> but no one uses
g-<move> if they've got <ctrl>-<move>. g-<move> isn't as nice. Not having
<crtl>-<move> (i.e. not fully supported) _is_ a problem! What is the
alternative to <ctrl>-<move> on the pc? (I.e. would <alt>-<move> work?)

[] I wouldn't complain if I had actually intended to hit the floating
[] eyes.  But in most of these cases I had actually been taking pains NOT...

The floating eye attack is particularly nasty (but the dungeon ain't no
picnic, either). Singling out the floating eye stills seems like an
idiosyncratic case to me. What about mimics? What about an option to
confirm all attacks (in the sense you mention, that is, once you say yes
then you can proceed to attack without question)? However to my way of
thinking this would have to be an all or nothing deal, _i_ can't justify
singling out the nasty eye.

[] A nymph seducing a female is pretty rediculous ...
[] ...
[] one turn, without seeming to have any problems with hitting through a
[] -3 AC, doesn't seem realistic.

First a minor point: Nymphs do not seduce females! They _charm_ them.

Okay, now the real point: An AC of -3 helps but isn't completely
"effective". You'll much prefer an AC below -10 or -15 or so. I don't put
much stock in the numbers (i.e. -3). Consider the whole scale (effectively)
-40 to +10 (you can do better). In this sense, -3 is only 26% of the
protection scale.

[] The only explanation I can divine for them having such ease is that
[] the characters aren't willing to prevent them.  Yet I'm quite good at
[] throwing darts at nymphs or striking them with sharp objects.

Exactly! Adjacent to you, a nymph is (mostly) irresistible. However,
there is a point where the nymphs don't seem to hit anymore (that goes
both ways as i mentioned before).

